From 99c4ba73dc211d803d7cf9a2350b180f79578d80 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 10:44:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Reconciliation credential (Option B): dedicated service
 accounts for the Matica outbox reconciliation job

Inventory API side of the agreed design - a true client-credentials
service identity, not a shared static key. Matica Service side (token
provider, OutboxEntry schema change, job update) follows separately.

New domain concept: PrintAgentServiceAccount - one row per Printer Agent
instance, scoped to a single tenant+branch (same granularity the
existing print-agent-token already uses). ClientId is a public GUID;
ClientSecretHash is verified via the existing IPasswordHasher, the same
interface already hashing tenant/admin passwords - not a new hashing
scheme. The raw secret is never persisted, only returned once at
provisioning time.

New token type: ReconciliationTokenOptions/IReconciliationTokenGenerator/
ReconciliationTokenGenerator mirror PrintAgentTokenGenerator's pattern
exactly - a third dedicated signing key, distinct from both the shared
tenant/admin key and the print-agent-token key, so a compromised branch
machine holding this key can only ever forge reconciliation-scoped
tokens. purpose claim shares its claim *type* with the print-agent
token (both "purpose") but a different *value* ("reconciliation" vs
"print-agent") - deliberately, since that's what lets print-result
accept either via RequireClaim's built-in multi-value support instead
of a custom IAuthorizationHandler.

Three new endpoints on AuthController:
- POST api/auth/service-token [AllowAnonymous] - client-credentials
  exchange, no user session involved. One error code (ServiceCredentialInvalid)
  whether the client id doesn't exist or the secret is wrong, matching
  the existing no-existence-leak convention; a distinct
  ServiceCredentialRevoked when the account exists and the secret
  matches but has been revoked.
- POST api/auth/service-accounts [SystemAdminOnly] - provisions a new
  account. Provisioning a standing credential is more consequential
  than a tenant self-serving an already-scoped print-agent token, hence
  the higher bar.
- POST api/auth/service-accounts/{id}/revoke [SystemAdminOnly] -
  idempotent, same posture as logout.

A real correctness issue caught and fixed during implementation, not
after: PrintFlowController had [Authorize(Policy=PrintAgentOnly)] at
the class level. ASP.NET Core combines multiple [Authorize] attributes
with AND semantics, not "most specific wins" - simply adding a second
policy on RecordPrintResult would have required BOTH to pass
simultaneously, silently breaking every reconciliation-token call
before it could ever succeed. Restructured to per-action [Authorize]
with no class-level attribute: ResolveForPrint keeps PrintAgentOnly
(it only ever runs during a live print attempt), RecordPrintResult gets
the new PrintResultAuthorized policy (accepts either token type, via
two AuthenticationSchemes entries plus RequireClaim's multi-value
support). Documented directly in the class's own doc comment so this
doesn't get "simplified" back to class-level by someone who doesn't
know why it matters.

Third JWT bearer scheme ("ReconciliationService") registered in
PresentationServiceRegistration.cs alongside the two existing ones.
IUnitOfWork/UnitOfWork/AppDbContext updated with the new repo/DbSet/
Fluent config, following the exact same pattern as every other entity
(RibbonType's config was the closest precedent - a simple lookup-
adjacent table with one unique index).

Migration not included, per established convention - no dotnet SDK in
the authoring environment. Run locally:
  Add-Migration Matica-Print-Flow-Reconciliation-Credential
  Update-Database
Expect one new table (PrintAgentServiceAccounts) with a unique index on
ClientId and non-unique indexes on TenantId/BranchId.

appsettings.json: new ReconciliationToken section (blank SigningKey,
same convention as Jwt/PrintAgentToken - must be a value distinct from
both of those, supplied via user-secrets/environment variable).

Verified by: brace balance across every touched and new file; JSON/XML
validity on appsettings.json and both resx files; resx key-count parity
between English and Arabic (163/163, four new entries added to each);
a repo-wide grep confirming every new symbol (PrintAgentServiceAccount,
ReconciliationToken*, ReconciliationAccessToken) is referenced only
where intended, with consistent naming across all ten touched files.
No dotnet SDK/NuGet access in the authoring environment - please build
and run the migration locally before merging.
